  │ Method │      Path       │                       Behavior                        │
  ├────────┼─────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mGET[39m    │ [38;5;153m/api/tasks[39m      │ list; optional [38;5;153m?status=[39m and [38;5;153m?assignee_id=[39m filters     │
  ├────────┼─────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mPOST[39m   │ [38;5;153m/api/tasks[39m      │ create; creator is the current user                   │
  ├────────┼─────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mGET[39m    │ [38;5;153m/api/tasks/{id}[39m │ single task                                           │
  ├────────┼─────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mPATCH[39m  │ [38;5;153m/api/tasks/{id}[39m │ partial update — title, description, status, assignee │
  ├────────┼─────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mDELETE[39m │ [38;5;153m/api/tasks/{id}[39m │ delete                                                │
  └────────┴─────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  [38;5;153mPATCH[39m rather than [38;5;153mPUT[39m deliberately: piece #3 wants to know [3mwhich fields[0m changed, and a partial-update payload carries
  that information natively. A whole-object [38;5;153mPUT[39m throws it away and forces a diff against the old row.

  [1mThe choke point.[0m Every mutating route funnels into one [38;5;153mapply_task_change(db, task, changes, actor)[39m in [38;5;153mroutes/tasks.py[39m.
  It validates, writes the fields, bumps [38;5;153mupdated_at[39m, and returns the set of fields that actually changed. Nothing else
  in the codebase writes to a task row. That single function is where piece #3 adds one line to emit an event — no
  archaeology required, no refactor of five call sites.

  [1mAuthorization[0m is intentionally flat for now: any logged-in user can read and modify any task. That's the right model
  for a small trusted team, it's what "shared tasks" implies, and it's much easier to tighten later than to loosen.
  Flagging it explicitly so it's a decision rather than an oversight.

  [1mFrontend.[0m [38;5;153mapp.js[39m fetches [38;5;153m/api/tasks[39m, renders a list, and handles create/edit/delete through the same API.
  Server-rendered login page, then a single task view. No framework — with this much UI, a framework is more setup than
  payoff.

  [1mData flow, end to end:[0m browser → [38;5;153mfetch[39m with session cookie → FastAPI dependency resolves current user (401 if absent)
  → route handler → [38;5;153mapply_task_change[39m → SQLAlchemy session commit → JSON response → JS re-renders the list.

  Does this section look right?

